  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of proton transport through a very narrow water-filled channel made from layered materials. A graphite base supports a structure that includes hexagonal boron nitride, with a slit-like channel in between. Inside this confined space, water molecules are arranged in an ordered row or network, suggesting that the geometry of the channel strongly organizes the water.\n\nA microscope tip sits above the device and creates an electric field, shown by curved dashed field lines extending down toward the channel. This field appears to influence the orientation or behavior of the water in the channel. Arrows at the side indicate movement along the channel, labeled as high H⁺ transport, implying that protons move efficiently through the confined water layer.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a mechanism in which nanoscale confinement and an externally applied electric field work together to promote fast proton conduction. The main scientific message is that water trapped in a two-dimensional channel between solid layers can support enhanced H⁺ transport, and that a nearby probe or tip can control or probe this process through its electric field.",
